<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bangladesh Liberation War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> Liberation War was the conflict of 1971 in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> won independence as Bangladesh. After the Pakistani army began a crackdown in March 1971, Bengali nationalist forces, the Mukti Bahini, fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war and some ten million refugees fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. India intervened in December, and on 16 December about 93,000 Pakistani troops surrendered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a>. Hundreds of thousands of people were killed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bangladesh-liberation-war.jpg" alt="Bangladesh Liberation War" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ExcelD24 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BangladeshLiberationWarMontage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> came into being in August 1947 in two geographically and culturally separate parts, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> between them. In 1948 Governor-General Muhammad Ali Jinnah declared that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> alone would be the federal language, although Bengali speakers made up over 56% of the population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> had the larger population but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a> dominated politically and received more from the common budget. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1970-bhola-cyclone">1970 Bhola cyclone</a> struck the East Pakistan coast on 12 November and killed an estimated 300,000 people, and eleven East Pakistani leaders charged the government with gross neglect.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the night of 25 March 1971 the Pakistan Army began Operation Searchlight, a planned pacification meant to take the major cities and eliminate all opposition within a month. Foreign journalists were deported beforehand, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-mujibur-rahman">Sheikh Mujibur Rahman</a> was arrested. Major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziaur-rahman">Ziaur Rahman</a> broadcast the declaration of independence from the radio station at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> on 26 March. Resistance grew as Bengali soldiers defected and merged into the Mukti Bahini, supplied from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, while Pakistan raised the Razakar, Al-Badr and Al-Shams militias.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> joined the war on 3 December after Pakistani air strikes on northern India. Indian aircraft achieved near-total air supremacy within a week, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> ports were blockaded. On 16 December 1971 the Pakistani commander in East Pakistan signed the Instrument of Surrender in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a>, and over 93,000 Pakistani troops surrendered, the largest surrender since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Pakistani military and supporting militias carried out widespread killings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> and rape. Estimates range from 300,000 to 3,000,000 killed and from 200,000 to 400,000 women raped. On 14 December 1971 the army and the Razakars seized at least 100 physicians, professors, writers and engineers in Dacca and murdered them. An estimated 10 million refugees fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and 30 million were internally displaced.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a> was the first state to recognise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, on 6 December 1971. The Simla Agreement of 1972 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> secured Pakistani recognition of Bangladesh in exchange for the return of prisoners of war. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a> the defeat discredited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-khan">Yahya Khan</a>'s rule. The war changed the geopolitical landscape of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> and became an episode of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> tension involving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bangladesh Liberation War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In December 1971 India intervened in the <b>Bangladesh Liberation War</b>, and within two weeks some 93,000 Pakistani soldiers surrendered at Dhaka, creating Bangladesh and confirming India's military primacy in South Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1971, during the <b>Bangladesh Liberation War</b>, the Provisional Government of Bangladesh adopted it as the national anthem; the first ten lines of the poem are the version usually sung.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amar-sonar-bangla">Amar Sonar Bangla</a></li></ul></section>
